window.AIBEMOCK={"n":1,"title":"Mock Test 1","theme":"Full syllabus","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"A balanced full-length paper across all the AIBE subjects, from Constitutional Law, CrPC/BNSS and CPC to Evidence, Family Law and Professional Ethics. A good first paper to find your baseline.","minutes":210,"neg":0,"pass":{"General / OBC":45,"SC / ST / PwD":40},"asOn":"19 November 2026","q":[{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"An application to set aside an arbitral award under Section 34 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 must be made within three months of receipt of the award. The court may entertain a delayed application for sufficient cause within a further period of","o":["fifteen days","thirty days","sixty days","ninety days"],"a":1,"e":"The proviso to Section 34(3) allows a further 30 days, but not beyond that. Section 5 of the Limitation Act cannot be used to extend this period."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"A right in rem, as distinguished from a right in personam, is a right","o":["available against the world at large","available only against members of a defined class","available only against the State","available only against a determinate person"],"a":0,"e":"Rights in rem, such as ownership, correspond to a duty on all persons not to interfere. Rights in personam, such as contractual rights, bind only specific persons."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 122 of the Evidence Act (now Section 128 BSA), a communication made to a married person by his or her spouse during marriage may be disclosed only","o":["with the written approval of the officer investigating the case","with the consent of the spouse who received the communication, in all cases","never, as the protection is absolute in all circumstances whatsoever","with the consent of the maker, or in proceedings between married persons"],"a":3,"e":"No married person can be compelled to disclose such communication, and the privilege is that of the maker. It can be waived only by the maker or his representative, and does not apply in suits between married persons or proceedings where one is prosecuted for a crime against the other."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 11 of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, for the adoption of a daughter, the adoptive parent must not have","o":["any child, whether natural or adopted, living at the time of adoption","a Hindu daughter's son who is living at the time of adoption","a Hindu daughter or son's daughter living at the time","any Hindu son who is living at the time of adoption"],"a":2,"e":"Section 11(iii) bars adoption of a daughter if the adopter already has a Hindu daughter or son's daughter, by blood or adoption, living. Section 11(i) similarly governs adoption of a son and refers to a living son, son's son or son's son's son."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Amardeep Singh v Harveen Kaur (2017), the Supreme Court held that the six-month waiting period under Section 13B(2) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 is","o":["directory, and may be waived by the court in fit cases","mandatory, and can never be waived by any court in any case","applicable only to the first motion and not to the second motion","applicable only to marriages that were solemnised before the year 1976"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the period is directory. It may be waived where the statutory separation period has run, mediation has failed, the parties have settled, and waiting would only prolong their agony. In Shilpa Sailesh v Varun Sreenivasan (2023), the Court also used Article 142 to dissolve marriages that had broken down irretrievably."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under the proviso to Section 23(2) of the BSA (earlier Section 27 Evidence Act), what part of an accused's statement made in police custody is admissible?","o":["Only the portion distinctly relating to the fact discovered","Nothing, since all custodial statements are wholly inadmissible in evidence","Only the portion that is exculpatory in nature for the accused","The entire statement, once the discovery has been proved in court"],"a":0,"e":"Section 27 is a proviso to the bar on custodial confessions. Only that part of the information which distinctly relates to the fact discovered is provable, whether or not it amounts to a confession (Pulukuri Kottaya v King-Emperor)."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Section 106 of the Evidence Act (now Section 109 BSA), which places the burden of proving a fact especially within a person's knowledge on that person,","o":["applies only to civil proceedings and never to any criminal trial","overrides the presumption of innocence in every criminal case","does not relieve the prosecution of its duty to prove its case","shifts the entire burden of proof onto the accused in all criminal trials"],"a":2,"e":"In Shambu Nath Mehra v State of Ajmer (1956), the Supreme Court held that the section does not absolve the prosecution. It applies only to facts which are especially within the accused's knowledge and not to the elements of the offence."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Under Article 38(1) of the Statute of the International Court of Justice, judicial decisions and the teachings of the most highly qualified publicists are","o":["binding precedents for all States before the Court","primary sources of international law, equal to treaties","conclusive evidence of the existence of customary law","subsidiary means for determining the rules of law"],"a":3,"e":"Article 38(1) lists treaties, custom and general principles of law as principal sources. Judicial decisions and writings of publicists are subsidiary means. Article 59 states that ICJ decisions bind only the parties to the case."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"A gift of immovable property under Section 123 of the Transfer of Property Act must be effected by","o":["a notarised affidavit executed by the donor alone, without any witnesses","a registered instrument signed by the donor and attested by two witnesses","an oral declaration made before two witnesses along with delivery of possession","an unregistered writing signed by both the donor and the donee"],"a":1,"e":"Section 123 requires a registered instrument, signed by the donor and attested by at least two witnesses, for immovable property. Gifts of movables may be made by registered instrument or delivery. Section 129 saves Muslim law gifts (hiba)."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"A conviction based on the uncorroborated testimony of an accomplice","o":["is permissible only in prosecutions for terrorism-related offences, and in no others","is illegal under the corresponding provision of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023","is valid only if the Magistrate has given permission to rely on such testimony","is not illegal, though courts ordinarily look for corroboration as a rule of prudence"],"a":3,"e":"Section 133 of the Evidence Act (now the corresponding BSA provision) says a conviction is not illegal merely because it rests on an accomplice's uncorroborated testimony. Illustration (b) to Section 114 permits the presumption that an accomplice is unworthy of credit unless corroborated, which becomes a rule of prudence."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"The rule that the proper plaintiff for a wrong done to a company is the company itself, and that courts will not ordinarily interfere in its internal management, comes from","o":["Foss v Harbottle (1843), the English Chancery case","Re Ooregum Gold Mining Co. of India (1892)","Salomon v Salomon & Co. Ltd. (1897)","Ashbury Railway Carriage Co. v Riche (1875)"],"a":0,"e":"Foss v Harbottle states the majority rule and proper plaintiff rule. Exceptions include fraud on the minority, ultra vires acts and oppression and mismanagement (now remedied under Sections 241-242 of the Companies Act, 2013). Salomon establishes separate legal personality."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 47 CPC, questions arising between the parties to the suit relating to the execution, discharge or satisfaction of a decree must be determined by","o":["the court executing the decree, and not by a separate suit","a separate suit filed before the court of first instance concerned","the Registrar of the High Court, on a written application made to him","the Appellate Court alone, on a reference made by the parties"],"a":0,"e":"Section 47 bars a separate suit for such questions. They are decided by the executing court, which also decides whether a person is a representative of a party. The order is treated as a decree and is appealable."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Which of the following CANNOT be proved by the opinion of an expert under Section 39 BSA (earlier Section 45 Evidence Act)?","o":["The identity of the finger impressions of a person","The meaning of a provision of Indian law","The identity of a person's handwriting","A point of foreign law arising in the case"],"a":1,"e":"Experts may give opinions on foreign law, science or art, and identity of handwriting or finger impressions. The interpretation of Indian law is the function of the court."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 176(3) of the BNSS, the visit of a forensic expert to the crime scene to collect forensic evidence is mandatory for offences punishable with imprisonment of","o":["five years or more","seven years or more","ten years or more","three years or more"],"a":1,"e":"Section 176(3) BNSS requires the officer in charge to cause a forensic expert to visit the crime scene and collect evidence in offences punishable with seven years or more, and the process must be videographed. It is a new provision with no CrPC equivalent."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Explanation IV to Section 11 CPC, any matter which might and ought to have been made a ground of defence or attack in the former suit is deemed to have been directly and substantially in issue. This rule is known as","o":["res sub judice under Section 10","estoppel by acquiescence or conduct","constructive res judicata (Explanation IV)","lis pendens under Section 52 of the TPA"],"a":2,"e":"Constructive res judicata bars a party from raising in a later suit a ground that could and should have been raised in the earlier one. Res sub judice (Section 10) bars parallel suits, and lis pendens concerns transfers during pendency of a suit."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three essential conditions for grant of a temporary injunction under Order XXXIX CPC?","o":["Balance of convenience in favour of the person applying","Pendency of a criminal complaint against the defendant","Irreparable injury if the injunction is refused by court","A prima facie case in favour of the person applying"],"a":1,"e":"A temporary injunction requires a prima facie case, balance of convenience, and irreparable injury that cannot be compensated in damages. The existence of criminal proceedings is not a condition."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 187(3) of the BNSS, an accused arrested for an offence punishable with death, life imprisonment or imprisonment of ten years or more becomes entitled to default bail if the investigation is not completed within","o":["sixty days of the commencement of detention","one hundred and twenty days of the commencement of detention","ninety days of the commencement of detention","one hundred and eighty days of the commencement of detention"],"a":2,"e":"The outer limit of detention for investigation is 90 days for offences punishable with death, life or at least ten years, and 60 days for other offences. The right to default (statutory) bail accrues on expiry if the charge-sheet is not filed and the accused is ready to furnish bail (formerly Section 167(2) CrPC)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 22(4), preventive detention for more than three months is valid only if, before that period expires, an Advisory Board reports sufficient cause. The Advisory Board must consist of persons who are","o":["qualified to be appointed as Judges of a High Court","members of the Legislature nominated by the Speaker","senior officers of the State Home Department","serving Judges of the Supreme Court of India only"],"a":0,"e":"Article 22(4)(a) requires the Advisory Board to consist of persons who are, or have been, or are qualified to be appointed as, Judges of a High Court. The 44th Amendment's reduction of the period to two months has never been brought into force."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"In Mohori Bibee v Dharmodas Ghose (1903), the Privy Council held that an agreement with a minor is","o":["valid, subject to ratification on attaining majority","valid but unenforceable against the minor","absolutely void from the very beginning (void ab initio)","voidable at the option of the minor"],"a":2,"e":"Under Sections 10 and 11 of the Indian Contract Act, a minor is not competent to contract. The agreement is void ab initio, cannot be ratified after majority, and there can be no estoppel against a minor. Contracts for necessaries are enforceable against the minor's property, not the minor personally."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which of the following statements about the writ jurisdiction of High Courts under Article 226 is correct?","o":["It lies only against bodies falling within the definition of 'State' in Article 12","It can be exercised for enforcing fundamental rights and for any other purpose","It is confined to enforcing fundamental rights alone, as under Article 32","It is itself a fundamental right, like the remedy under Article 32"],"a":1,"e":"Article 226 empowers High Courts to issue writs to any person or authority, including the Government, for enforcement of fundamental rights 'and for any other purpose'. Unlike Article 32, which is itself a fundamental right, the remedy under Article 226 is discretionary."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"The maxim 'nemo judex in causa sua' embodies which principle of natural justice?","o":["The right of appeal to a higher forum","The right to a fair hearing","The rule against bias in the decision-maker","The duty to record reasons"],"a":2,"e":"'No one should be a judge in his own cause' is the rule against bias, which includes pecuniary, personal and subject-matter bias. The test is a reasonable likelihood of bias. The right to a fair hearing is audi alteram partem."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Sushila Aggarwal v State (NCT of Delhi) (2020), the Constitution Bench held that a direction granting anticipatory bail","o":["need not be time-limited and can last till the end of the trial","ends automatically as soon as the charge-sheet is filed in the court","lapses when the Magistrate summons the accused to appear before him","remains valid for a maximum period of thirty days at a single time"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that protection from arrest does not end at any fixed stage such as summoning or filing of charge-sheet, though courts may limit the protection in appropriate cases. The provision is now Section 482 BNSS."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 20 of the BNS (corresponding to Section 82 of the IPC), nothing is an offence which is done by a child under the age of","o":["twelve years","fourteen years","ten years","seven years"],"a":3,"e":"A child under seven is conclusively presumed incapable of crime (doli incapax). Under Section 21 BNS (IPC Section 83), an act by a child above seven and under twelve is not an offence if he has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"In Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal (2020), the Supreme Court held that the certificate under Section 65B(4) of the Evidence Act (now Section 63 BSA) is","o":["necessary even where the original device itself is produced and proved in court, without exception","merely directory, and can be dispensed with at the court's discretion in all cases and all trials","required only in criminal cases, and never required in civil proceedings of any kind whatsoever","a mandatory condition for secondary electronic evidence, but not if the original device is produced"],"a":3,"e":"The Court overruled Shafhi Mohammad and held that the certificate is a pre-condition for secondary electronic records, such as printouts or copies. Where the original device is produced and proved, no certificate is needed. Section 63 BSA retains the requirement and prescribes a certificate format."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"The Income-tax Act, 2025, which replaced the Income-tax Act, 1961 from 1 April 2026, substitutes the concepts of 'previous year' and 'assessment year' with","o":["a single 'tax year'","a single 'financial year'","a single 'return year'","a single 'accounting year'"],"a":0,"e":"The Income-tax Act, 2025 replaces the dual system with a single 'Tax Year' of twelve months from 1 April. Income earned up to 31 March 2026 continues to be governed by the 1961 Act."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following arrangements is permissible for an advocate under the Bar Council of India Rules on professional standards?","o":["Charging a fixed fee that does not depend on the outcome of the case","Buying property sold in execution of a decree in a case he handled","Agreeing to a fee that is contingent on the result of the litigation","Sharing his professional fee with a person who is not an advocate"],"a":0,"e":"The BCI Rules (Part VI, Chapter II) bar contingent fees and sharing in the proceeds of litigation, bar an advocate from bidding for or buying property sold in execution in a matter in which he was engaged, and bar fee-sharing with non-advocates. A fixed fee unrelated to the outcome is permitted."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Which statement about a dying declaration (Section 26 BSA, earlier Section 32(1) Evidence Act) is correct?","o":["It must always be corroborated by other evidence as a rule of law","It can be the sole basis of conviction if found reliable","It must be in writing and be signed by the person who made it","It is admissible only if it has been recorded by a Magistrate"],"a":1,"e":"A dying declaration inspiring confidence can sustain a conviction without corroboration (Khushal Rao v State of Bombay). It may be oral, and it need not be recorded by a Magistrate, though the maker's fitness to make the statement must be established."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 149(3) of the Companies Act, 2013, every company must have at least one director who has stayed in India for a total period of not less than","o":["ninety days in the immediately preceding calendar year","one hundred and eighty-two days in the previous calendar year","two hundred and forty days in the immediately preceding calendar year","one hundred and twenty days in the immediately preceding calendar year"],"a":1,"e":"The resident director requirement is 182 days in the previous calendar year. For a company newly incorporated, the requirement applies proportionately for the financial year of incorporation."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 183 of the BNSS (earlier Section 164 CrPC), a confession or statement during investigation can be recorded by","o":["a Judicial or Metropolitan Magistrate, whether or not he has jurisdiction","only the Magistrate having territorial jurisdiction over the case concerned","only the Sessions Judge of the Sessions Division concerned with the case","a police officer not below the rank of Superintendent of Police"],"a":0,"e":"Any Metropolitan or Judicial Magistrate may record a confession, whether or not he has jurisdiction. He must first explain that the person is not bound to confess and satisfy himself that it is voluntary. BNSS also permits audio-video recording."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"Under the Consumer Protection (Jurisdiction of the District Commission, the State Commission and the National Commission) Rules, 2021, a District Commission can entertain a complaint where the value of goods or services paid as consideration does not exceed","o":["Rs. 20 lakh","Rs. 2 crore","Rs. 1 crore","Rs. 50 lakh"],"a":3,"e":"Pecuniary jurisdiction under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 is based on consideration paid. The District Commission can hear cases up to Rs. 50 lakh, the State Commission above Rs. 50 lakh and up to Rs. 2 crore, and the National Commission above Rs. 2 crore."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which decision of a nine-judge Bench of the Supreme Court recognised the right to privacy as a fundamental right under Article 21?","o":["Suresh Kumar Koushal v Naz Foundation (2013)","Navtej Singh Johar v Union of India (2018)","Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v Union of India (2017)","Common Cause v Union of India (2018), the living will case"],"a":2,"e":"Puttaswamy unanimously held that privacy is an intrinsic part of life and personal liberty under Article 21 and of the freedoms in Part III. It overruled M.P. Sharma and the contrary part of Kharak Singh."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"An award made by a Lok Adalat under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 is","o":["merely recommendatory unless approved by the court","appealable to the High Court within thirty days","deemed a decree of a civil court and final, with no appeal","enforceable only after registration with the Legal Services Authority"],"a":2,"e":"Section 21 treats a Lok Adalat award as a civil court decree, final and binding on all parties, and not appealable. Parties can only seek relief by a writ petition on limited grounds. Court fee paid is refunded."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is a recognised ground for review of a decree or order under Order XLVII Rule 1 CPC?","o":["A different view being possible on re-appreciation of the evidence","Dissatisfaction with the reasoning adopted by the court","A change in the personal circumstances of the losing party","A mistake or error apparent on the face of the record"],"a":3,"e":"Review lies on discovery of new and important matter or evidence not within the applicant's knowledge despite due diligence, a mistake or error apparent on the face of the record, or any other sufficient reason. Re-appreciation of evidence is the province of an appeal, not review."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT a ground on which reasonable restrictions on freedom of speech and expression can be imposed under Article 19(2)?","o":["Contempt of court proceedings","Defamation of a private person","Protection of public health","Decency or morality in society"],"a":2,"e":"Article 19(2) lists sovereignty and integrity of India, security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency or morality, contempt of court, defamation and incitement to an offence. Public health is not a listed ground."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"A decision that is so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could ever have come to it is open to judicial review on the ground of","o":["promissory estoppel","legitimate expectation","Wednesbury unreasonableness","colourable exercise of power"],"a":2,"e":"The Wednesbury principle (Associated Provincial Picture Houses v Wednesbury Corporation, 1948) allows interference with decisions so absurd that no sensible authority could have reached them. Indian courts also apply proportionality in appropriate cases."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Which of the following forms of punishment has been newly introduced by the BNS, 2023 for certain petty offences and was not available under the Indian Penal Code, 1860?","o":["Imprisonment for life","Community service as a form of punishment","Solitary confinement","Rigorous imprisonment"],"a":1,"e":"Section 4 BNS adds community service to the list of punishments, prescribed for offences such as petty theft, defamation and attempt to commit suicide to compel or restrain a public servant. Solitary confinement, rigorous imprisonment and life imprisonment existed under the IPC."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"For a breach of promise to amount to the offence of cheating under Section 318 of the BNS, the dishonest intention of the accused must be shown to have existed","o":["at the time the promise was breached","at any time during the subsistence of the contract","at the time the complaint was lodged","at the very time when the promise was made"],"a":3,"e":"A mere breach of contract is a civil wrong. Cheating requires fraudulent or dishonest inducement from the very inception, which is inferred from circumstances at the time of the promise."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"The concept of the 'Grundnorm', the ultimate basic norm from which the validity of all other norms in a legal system is derived, is associated with","o":["H.L.A. Hart","John Austin","Roscoe Pound","Hans Kelsen"],"a":3,"e":"Kelsen's Pure Theory of Law arranges norms in a hierarchy whose validity traces back to the Grundnorm. Hart's comparable idea is the 'rule of recognition', and Austin saw law as the command of the sovereign."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Under Article 9 of the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961, the receiving State may declare a member of a diplomatic mission persona non grata","o":["only after a ruling of the International Court of Justice","at any time, without having to explain its decision","only with the consent of the Security Council","only after giving thirty days' prior notice"],"a":1,"e":"The receiving State may at any time and without explanation notify the sending State that a diplomat is persona non grata. The sending State must then recall the person or terminate his functions, failing which the receiving State may refuse to recognise him as a member of the mission."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Vineeta Sharma v Rakesh Sharma (2020), the Supreme Court held that a daughter's coparcenary right under amended Section 6 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956","o":["applies only to property that was acquired after 9 September 2005 by the family","arises only if the father was alive on 9 September 2005 itself, not otherwise","arises only if the daughter was unmarried on that date in the year 2005","is by birth and does not need the father to be alive on 9 September 2005"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the coparcenary right is by birth, like a son's, and the 2005 amendment is retrospective in operation. It overruled Prakash v Phulavati to the extent of requiring the father to be alive on that date."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"After the 2018 amendment to the Specific Relief Act, 1963, specific performance of a contract is","o":["available only where monetary compensation is inadequate","barred where the contract concerns immovable property","the general rule, subject to the statutory exceptions","wholly discretionary, as it was before the amendment"],"a":2,"e":"The amended Section 10 says specific performance shall be enforced subject to Sections 11(2), 14 and 16. It is no longer a discretionary remedy as under the earlier Section 20."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"In Ex-Capt. Harish Uppal v Union of India (2002), the Supreme Court held that advocates","o":["may strike but must give prior notice to the Registrar of the court","have no right to strike or boycott courts, and doing so is illegal","may strike for up to three days in a year in the interest of the Bar","may boycott courts whenever the Bar Association passes a resolution"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that lawyers have no right to strike or give a call for boycott, even a token strike. Only in the rarest of rare cases, where the dignity or independence of the Bar or Bench is at stake, may the Chief Justice permit abstention for a day. Strikes can amount to professional misconduct and contempt."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"In a summary suit under Order XXXVII CPC, the defendant can defend the suit only with leave of the court, which must be applied for within how many days of service of summons for judgment?","o":["seven days","ten days","fifteen days","thirty days"],"a":1,"e":"Under Order XXXVII Rule 3(5), the defendant must enter appearance and apply for leave to defend within ten days of service of summons for judgment. Otherwise the plaintiff is entitled to a decree."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"For acquisition of land for a private company, the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013 requires the prior consent of at least what proportion of the affected families?","o":["ninety per cent","seventy per cent","fifty-one per cent","eighty per cent"],"a":3,"e":"Section 2(2) requires 80% consent for private companies and 70% for public-private partnership projects. Government acquisitions for public purposes do not require such consent."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Section 66C of the Information Technology Act, 2000 punishes identity theft with imprisonment of up to three years and fine of up to","o":["Rs. 2 lakh","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 1 lakh","Rs. 5 lakh"],"a":2,"e":"Section 66C punishes the fraudulent or dishonest use of another person's electronic signature, password or other unique identification feature. Section 66D punishes cheating by personation using a computer resource."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 16 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, a child born of a marriage that is void under Section 11 is","o":["legitimate only if the marriage is annulled within one year","illegitimate for all purposes","legitimate, whether or not a decree of nullity is granted","legitimate only for purposes of the mother's property"],"a":2,"e":"Section 16(1) deems such children legitimate. Under Section 16(3), their rights in property are generally limited to that of their parents, and the Supreme Court in Revanasiddappa v Mallikarjun (2023) held that they are entitled to their parents' share in property."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"A second appeal to the High Court under Section 100 CPC lies only if the High Court is satisfied that the case involves","o":["a grave question of fact","a substantial question of law","any error in appreciation of evidence","a question of general public importance"],"a":1,"e":"Section 100 confines second appeals to substantial questions of law, which must be formulated by the High Court. Concurrent findings of fact are ordinarily not disturbed (Santosh Hazari v Purushottam Tiwari, 2001)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which decision established that Articles 14, 19 and 21 are not mutually exclusive and that the procedure under Article 21 must be just, fair and reasonable?","o":["Maneka Gandhi v Union of India (1978)","A.K. Gopalan v State of Madras (1950)","A.D.M. Jabalpur v Shivkant Shukla (1976)","Kharak Singh v State of Uttar Pradesh (1962)"],"a":0,"e":"Maneka Gandhi overruled the narrow reading in A.K. Gopalan and held that 'procedure established by law' must satisfy the tests of Articles 14 and 19. This created the 'golden triangle' of Articles 14, 19 and 21."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Social Security, 2020, a fixed-term employee becomes eligible for gratuity on completing","o":["five years of continuous service","one year of continuous service","two years of continuous service","three years of continuous service"],"a":1,"e":"Other employees remain subject to the five-year requirement, though the Code allows for relaxation in cases of death or disablement. Fixed-term employees receive gratuity on a pro rata basis after one year."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Kihoto Hollohan v Zachillhu (1992), the Constitution Bench held that a decision of the Speaker on disqualification under the Tenth Schedule is","o":["final and immune from any judicial scrutiny whatsoever in all cases","appealable on its merits to the President of India within thirty days","open to limited judicial review, as for mala fides or perversity","reviewable only by the Election Commission of India on a reference"],"a":2,"e":"The Court upheld the Tenth Schedule but struck down paragraph 7, which ousted court jurisdiction. The Speaker acts as a tribunal, and his decision is open to limited judicial review for jurisdictional errors, mala fides, perversity and violation of constitutional mandate."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 80(2) of the BNS, the minimum punishment for dowry death is","o":["not less than five years, extendable to life imprisonment","not less than ten years, extendable to life imprisonment","a term up to ten years, along with a fine only, at the court's option","not less than seven years, extendable to life imprisonment"],"a":3,"e":"Section 80 BNS corresponds to Section 304B IPC. It applies to the death of a woman within seven years of marriage where, soon before her death, she was subjected to cruelty or harassment for dowry."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020 (in force since 21 November 2025), prior government permission for lay-off, retrenchment and closure is required in industrial establishments having at least","o":["two hundred workers","one hundred workers","four hundred workers","three hundred workers"],"a":3,"e":"The Code raised the threshold from 100 workers (Chapter VB of the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947) to 300 workers. The appropriate Government may raise it further by notification."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is NOT a mode of out-of-court settlement of disputes mentioned in Section 89 CPC?","o":["Judicial settlement through Lok Adalat","Summary suit under Order XXXVII","Mediation","Conciliation"],"a":1,"e":"Section 89(2) lists arbitration, conciliation, judicial settlement including settlement through Lok Adalat, and mediation. A summary suit is a form of adjudication by a court, not an ADR mode."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Arnesh Kumar v State of Bihar (2014), the Supreme Court directed that the police should not automatically arrest an accused in cases where the offence is punishable with imprisonment for a term that","o":["may be less than five years only, with or without any fine","may be less than three years only, with or without any fine","may extend to ten years or more, with or without any fine","may be less than seven years or may extend to seven years"],"a":3,"e":"The Court directed that police should satisfy themselves, with reasons, on the necessity of arrest and ordinarily issue a notice of appearance (Section 41A CrPC, now Section 35(3) BNSS). Magistrates must not authorise detention mechanically."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"A classification under Article 14 is valid only if it is based on an intelligible differentia and that differentia","o":["relates to geographical territories alone and not to persons","has a rational nexus with the object sought to be achieved by the law","applies equally to all persons without any distinction at all","is approved by a two-thirds majority of the legislature concerned"],"a":1,"e":"The twin test of permissible classification (State of West Bengal v Anwar Ali Sarkar; Ram Krishna Dalmia) requires an intelligible differentia and a rational nexus to the object. Article 14 forbids class legislation, not reasonable classification. Arbitrariness is a further ground of invalidity (E.P. Royappa)."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a sale of tangible immovable property of the value of one hundred rupees and upwards can be made only","o":["by an unregistered deed attested by two witnesses","by an oral agreement accompanied by delivery of possession","by mere delivery of title deeds without any written instrument","by a registered instrument executed by the seller"],"a":3,"e":"For tangible immovable property worth Rs. 100 or more, sale requires a registered instrument. Only for property worth less than Rs. 100 may sale be by delivery of possession. Delivery of title deeds without writing creates only an equitable mortgage in notified towns."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Which of the following correctly states the law on consideration under the Indian Contract Act, 1872?","o":["It must always be adequate in terms of its monetary value","Past consideration is never a valid consideration in Indian law","It may move from the promisee or any other person","It must move only from the promisee, as under English law"],"a":2,"e":"Section 2(d) allows consideration from the promisee 'or any other person', and recognises past, present and future consideration. Inadequacy of consideration does not invalidate a contract (Explanation 2 to Section 25), though consideration must be real."},{"s":"Limitation Law","q":"Section 5 of the Limitation Act, 1963, which allows extension of the prescribed period on showing sufficient cause, does NOT apply to","o":["an application to set aside an ex parte decree","an application for leave to appeal","a suit, as distinct from an appeal or an application","an appeal"],"a":2,"e":"Section 5 applies to appeals and applications (other than applications under Order XXI CPC), but not to suits. A suit filed after the prescribed period is barred, and the court must dismiss it under Section 3 even if limitation has not been pleaded."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 63 of the BNS, sexual intercourse with a woman is rape irrespective of her consent if she is under the age of","o":["eighteen years","twelve years of age","fifteen years of age","sixteen years of age"],"a":0,"e":"Consent is immaterial if the woman is under eighteen. The marital exception applies only where the wife is not under eighteen, in line with Independent Thought v Union of India (2017)."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following punishments can the Disciplinary Committee of a State Bar Council NOT impose on an advocate found guilty of professional misconduct under Section 35 of the Advocates Act, 1961?","o":["Reprimanding the advocate through a formal written order","Removing the advocate's name from the State roll of advocates","Suspending the advocate from practice for a stated period","Imposing a monetary fine as a disciplinary penalty"],"a":3,"e":"Section 35(3) allows the Disciplinary Committee to dismiss the complaint, reprimand the advocate, suspend him from practice for a period, or remove his name from the State roll. A monetary fine is not among the penalties."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 80 CPC, a suit against the Government cannot be instituted until the expiry of how long after notice has been delivered?","o":["three months","six months","one month","two months"],"a":3,"e":"Section 80(1) requires two months' prior notice. Under Section 80(2), the court may allow a suit for urgent or immediate relief without the notice, but must give the Government a reasonable opportunity to show cause before granting final relief."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Which Constitution Amendment Act inserted Articles 246A and 279A and introduced the Goods and Services Tax regime?","o":["The Constitution (101st Amendment) Act, 2016","The Constitution (100th Amendment) Act, 2015","The Constitution (103rd Amendment) Act, 2019","The Constitution (102nd Amendment) Act, 2018"],"a":0,"e":"The 101st Amendment created the GST framework and the GST Council (Article 279A). The 100th concerned exchange of enclaves with Bangladesh, the 102nd gave constitutional status to the NCBC, and the 103rd introduced EWS reservation."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Which of the following CANNOT be the subject of theft under Section 303 of the BNS?","o":["Standing timber after it is severed from the earth","A gold chain worn by a person who is asleep","Harvested grain lying in the owner's field","Land, as it is immovable property attached to the earth"],"a":3,"e":"Theft requires dishonest removal of movable property out of the possession of a person without consent. Things attached to the earth are not movable property until severed. Land itself cannot be stolen."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is NOT a ground for rejection of a plaint under Order VII Rule 11 CPC?","o":["The defendant has a strong defence on the merits of the case","The relief is undervalued and the valuation is not corrected in time","The suit appears from the plaint itself to be barred by law","The plaint does not disclose a cause of action"],"a":0,"e":"Rejection of a plaint under Order VII Rule 11 is decided only on the plaint's averments and documents. The defendant's defence is not considered at that stage (Saleem Bhai v State of Maharashtra, 2003)."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Which of the following persons is NOT entitled to claim maintenance under Section 144 of the BNSS (earlier Section 125 CrPC)?","o":["A legitimate or illegitimate minor child unable to maintain itself","A divorced wife who has not remarried and cannot maintain herself","A wife who is living in adultery, unless the husband has condoned it","A father or mother who is unable to maintain himself or herself"],"a":2,"e":"Section 144 entitles a wife unable to maintain herself, minor children (legitimate or illegitimate), adult children with a physical or mental disability, and parents. A wife is disqualified if she lives in adultery, refuses without sufficient reason to live with her husband, or they live separately by mutual consent."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Shayara Bano v Union of India (2017), the Supreme Court by majority held that talaq-e-biddat (instant triple talaq) is","o":["valid, but only after a mandatory attempt at arbitration","invalid and unconstitutional, as it is manifestly arbitrary","valid, but only if pronounced in the presence of two witnesses","valid, being an essential practice of Islam protected by Article 25"],"a":1,"e":"By a 3:2 majority the practice was set aside. Parliament later enacted the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019, which makes pronouncing instant triple talaq a criminal offence."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"An assembly of persons is an 'unlawful assembly' under Section 189 of the BNS (earlier Section 141 IPC) only if it consists of at least how many persons sharing a common object?","o":["ten persons","three persons","five persons","two persons"],"a":2,"e":"Unlawful assembly requires five or more persons with one of the common objects listed in the section. Every member of such an assembly is constructively liable for offences committed in prosecution of the common object (Section 190 BNS, earlier Section 149 IPC)."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under the Trade Marks Act, 1999, the registration of a trade mark is valid for an initial period of","o":["seven years, renewable for successive periods of seven years","ten years, and not renewable thereafter","ten years, renewable for successive periods of ten years","twenty years, renewable for successive periods of ten years"],"a":2,"e":"Section 25 provides that registration lasts ten years from the date of application and may be renewed indefinitely for further ten-year periods on payment of the prescribed fee."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 12 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, the maximum punishment for contempt of court is","o":["simple imprisonment up to six months, or fine up to Rs. 2,000, or both","simple imprisonment up to one year, or fine up to Rs. 2,000, or both","simple imprisonment up to three months, or fine up to Rs. 1,000, or both","simple imprisonment up to six months, or fine up to Rs. 5,000, or both"],"a":0,"e":"Section 12(1) provides simple imprisonment up to six months, fine up to Rs. 2,000, or both. The accused may be discharged or the punishment remitted on a bona fide apology to the satisfaction of the court."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"According to SCG Contracts India Pvt. Ltd. v K.S. Chamankar Infrastructure Pvt. Ltd. (2019), the outer limit for filing a written statement in an ordinary civil suit (not a commercial suit) is","o":["one hundred and fifty days from the date of service of summons","ninety days from the date of service of summons","one hundred and twenty days from the date of service of summons","sixty days from the date of service of summons"],"a":1,"e":"Order VIII Rule 1 requires a written statement within 30 days, extendable by the court up to 90 days from service of summons. The Court held that the court cannot extend time beyond 90 days. For commercial suits the outer limit is 120 days."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"In Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v Union of India (1996), the Supreme Court held that the 'precautionary principle' and the 'polluter pays principle' are","o":["Directive Principles that cannot be enforced by any court at all","applicable only to the industries specified under the Water Act","part of the environmental law of India, flowing from Article 21","mere persuasive guidelines of foreign courts without any binding force"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that sustainable development, the precautionary principle and the polluter pays principle are part of Indian environmental law. The burden of proving that an activity is environmentally benign lies on the developer or industry."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following is NOT a qualification for being admitted as an advocate on a State roll under Section 24 of the Advocates Act, 1961?","o":["Having worked for at least two years as a junior to a senior advocate","Holding a degree in law from a university recognised by the BCI","Being a citizen of India, subject to reciprocity rules for foreign nationals","Having completed twenty-one years of age on the date of application"],"a":0,"e":"Section 24(1) requires citizenship (with a reciprocity exception), completion of 21 years, a recognised law degree and compliance with the conditions in the State Bar Council's rules. Apprenticeship under a senior is not a statutory condition. The AIBE is a separate requirement for the Certificate of Practice."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"The protection of confidential communications between an advocate and client (Section 126 of the Evidence Act, now Section 132 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam),","o":["continues even after the engagement with the client has ended","lapses upon the death of the client in all circumstances","applies only while a suit or proceeding is actually pending in court","lapses once the professional relationship has been terminated by either side"],"a":0,"e":"The privilege belongs to the client and survives the end of the engagement. It can be lost only by the client's express consent, or where the communication is made in furtherance of an illegal purpose, or where the advocate observes facts showing a crime or fraud since the engagement began."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In which case did a thirteen-judge Bench of the Supreme Court hold, by a narrow majority, that Parliament's power to amend under Article 368 does not extend to altering the 'basic structure' of the Constitution?","o":["Golak Nath v State of Punjab (1967), by an eleven-judge Bench","Kesavananda Bharati v State of Kerala (1973)","Indira Nehru Gandhi v Raj Narain (1975)","Minerva Mills Ltd. v Union of India (1980)"],"a":1,"e":"Kesavananda Bharati (7:6) overruled Golak Nath's blanket bar on amending Fundamental Rights, but held that the amending power cannot destroy the Constitution's basic structure. Later cases such as Indira Gandhi v Raj Narain and Minerva Mills applied the doctrine."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"The doctrine of part performance under Section 53A of the Transfer of Property Act can be invoked by a transferee in possession only as","o":["a method of acquiring a valid title without any writing at all","a defence against the transferor's attempt to disturb possession","a ground to compel the transferor to register the document","a basis for filing a suit to obtain full ownership of the property"],"a":1,"e":"Section 53A is a shield and not a sword. It protects the transferee in possession, who has performed or is willing to perform his part of a written contract, from eviction by the transferor. It does not confer title."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under the Advocates Act, 1961, an appeal against an order of the Disciplinary Committee of the Bar Council of India lies to","o":["the Supreme Court, within sixty days of the order being communicated","the Central Government, through the Ministry of Law and Justice","the High Court having jurisdiction over the advocate's place of practice","a Review Committee constituted by the concerned State Bar Council"],"a":0,"e":"Appeals from a State Bar Council's Disciplinary Committee go to the Bar Council of India (Section 37). A further appeal from the BCI's Disciplinary Committee lies to the Supreme Court within 60 days (Section 38)."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 479 of the BNSS, an undertrial who is a first-time offender (never previously convicted) is entitled to be released on bond after undergoing detention for what portion of the maximum sentence for the offence?","o":["one-fourth of the maximum sentence","one-half of the maximum sentence","two-thirds of the maximum sentence","one-third of the maximum sentence"],"a":3,"e":"Section 479 (earlier Section 436A CrPC) gives first-time offenders release after one-third of the maximum term, and other undertrials after one-half. It does not apply to offences punishable with death or life imprisonment. The Supreme Court has directed that it benefits eligible undertrials irrespective of when the case was registered."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"The rule of absolute liability, under which an enterprise engaged in a hazardous activity is liable without any exceptions, was laid down in","o":["Donoghue v Stevenson (1932), the snail in the bottle case","Union Carbide Corporation v Union of India (1989), the settlement case","M.C. Mehta v Union of India (1987), the Oleum gas leak case","Rylands v Fletcher (1868), the English House of Lords case"],"a":2,"e":"M.C. Mehta evolved absolute liability, which has no exceptions and applies to enterprises engaged in hazardous or inherently dangerous activities. Rylands v Fletcher laid down strict liability subject to exceptions."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under the BSA (Section 57, earlier Section 62 of the Evidence Act), 'primary evidence' means","o":["a copy compared with the original by a competent person","a certified copy of it issued by a public officer","an oral account of its contents by a person who has seen it","the document itself produced for inspection of the Court"],"a":3,"e":"Primary evidence is the document itself. Certified copies, compared copies and oral accounts are secondary evidence. The BSA expressly treats an electronic record as a document."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Under the General Clauses Act, 1897, the word 'month' means","o":["a month reckoned by the British calendar","a period of exactly thirty days from the date","a lunar month from new moon to new moon","a period of four weeks from the date"],"a":0,"e":"Section 3(35) defines a 'month' as a month reckoned according to the British (Gregorian) calendar. A 'year' means a year reckoned the same way."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 16 of the Advocates Act, 1961, which authority may designate an advocate as a Senior Advocate?","o":["The Bar Council of India, on an application made by the advocate concerned","The State Bar Council, on the recommendation of the Advocate General","The Supreme Court or a High Court, with the advocate's consent","The Central Government, on the written advice of the Attorney General"],"a":2,"e":"Section 16(2) empowers the Supreme Court or a High Court to designate an advocate, with his consent, if by ability, standing at the Bar or special knowledge or experience in law he deserves the distinction. Indira Jaising v Supreme Court (2017) laid down the guidelines."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"An easement by prescription under Section 15 of the Indian Easements Act, 1882 is acquired through peaceable and open enjoyment as of right, without interruption, for","o":["twenty years","fifteen years","twelve years","thirty years"],"a":0,"e":"Section 15 requires 20 years of uninterrupted enjoyment for an easement over private property, and 30 years where the servient land belongs to the Government. Twelve years is the limitation for possession of immovable property."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"An Ordinance promulgated by the President under Article 123 ceases to operate, unless disapproved earlier, at the expiration of how long from the reassembly of Parliament?","o":["four weeks from the reassembly of Parliament","three months from the reassembly of Parliament","eight weeks from the reassembly of Parliament","six weeks from the reassembly of Parliament"],"a":3,"e":"Under Article 123(2), an Ordinance must be laid before both Houses and ceases to operate six weeks after Parliament reassembles, or earlier if both Houses pass resolutions disapproving it. Since Parliament cannot go more than six months between sessions, an Ordinance can last at most about six months and six weeks."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the proviso to Section 173(3) of the BNSS, for a cognizable offence punishable with imprisonment of three years or more but less than seven years, the officer in charge may, with prior permission of a DSP-rank officer, hold a preliminary enquiry within","o":["thirty days","seven days","fourteen days","twenty-one days"],"a":2,"e":"Section 173(3) BNSS allows a preliminary enquiry within 14 days to ascertain whether a prima facie case exists in such offences. This codifies the limited exception to mandatory registration recognised in Lalita Kumari v Government of U.P. (2014). BNSS also permits e-FIRs and zero FIRs."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 103(2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, when a group of five or more persons acting in concert commits murder on the ground of race, caste, community, sex, place of birth, language or personal belief, each member of the group is punishable with","o":["death or imprisonment for life, and also fine","simple imprisonment up to seven years, or fine","imprisonment for a term between seven and fourteen years","rigorous imprisonment up to ten years, and fine"],"a":0,"e":"Section 103(2) BNS is the new offence of murder by a group (mob lynching). Each member is liable to death or life imprisonment and fine. Murder under Section 101 is punished under Section 103(1)."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under Section 22 of the Copyright Act, 1957, the term of copyright in a published original literary work is","o":["twenty-five years from first publication","the lifetime of the author plus sixty years","the lifetime of the author plus fifty years","the lifetime of the author plus seventy years"],"a":1,"e":"The term is the author's life and sixty years from the beginning of the calendar year following the year of death. Fifty years was the international minimum before the 1999 amendment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which decision of the Supreme Court held that a Presidential Proclamation under Article 356 is subject to judicial review and that secularism is part of the basic structure?","o":["Shamsher Singh v State of Punjab (1974)","S.R. Bommai v Union of India (1994), by a nine-judge Bench","State of Rajasthan v Union of India (1977)","Raja Ram Pal v Hon'ble Speaker, Lok Sabha (2007)"],"a":1,"e":"Bommai, decided by a nine-judge Bench, held that the satisfaction of the President under Article 356 can be reviewed for mala fides or irrelevant material, that a floor test is the proper way to test majority, and that secularism is part of the basic structure."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"A statutory appeal against an award, decision or order of the National Green Tribunal under Section 22 of the NGT Act, 2010 lies to","o":["a Full Bench of the National Green Tribunal","the High Court of the concerned State","the Central Government, in the Ministry of Environment","the Supreme Court of India, within ninety days"],"a":3,"e":"Section 22 provides an appeal to the Supreme Court within ninety days. The Supreme Court has also held that constitutional writ remedies before High Courts are not wholly ousted."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, a worker in an industrial establishment cannot go on strike without giving notice of strike at least","o":["thirty days before the strike","sixty days before the strike","fourteen days before the strike","forty-five days before the strike"],"a":1,"e":"The Code extends the notice requirement to all industrial establishments, which earlier applied only to public utility services. The strike must also not begin within 14 days of giving notice, or before the date specified in the notice."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 29A of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, the parties may by consent extend the period for making an award in a domestic arbitration by a period not exceeding","o":["three months","six months","nine months","twelve months"],"a":1,"e":"An award must be made within twelve months from completion of pleadings. The parties may extend this by up to six months by consent. Any further extension requires an order of the court on sufficient cause."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Githa Hariharan v Reserve Bank of India (1999), the Supreme Court interpreted the word 'after' in Section 6(a) of the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act, 1956 to mean","o":["only after the death of the father and not before that","only after the child has attained twelve years of age","in the absence of the father, not only after his death","only after the father has remarried another woman"],"a":2,"e":"To preserve the constitutionality of Section 6(a) and ensure gender equality, the Court read 'after' as 'in the absence of'. The mother can act as the natural guardian when the father is absent, indifferent or otherwise not in a position to act."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Which provision of the Information Technology Act, 2000 was struck down as violating Article 19(1)(a) in Shreya Singhal v Union of India (2015)?","o":["Section 66A","Section 66F","Section 69A","Section 43A"],"a":0,"e":"Section 66A (punishment for sending offensive messages) was struck down as vague and overbroad. Section 69A (blocking) was upheld, and Section 79 was read down so that 'actual knowledge' means a court order or government notification."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"The inherent powers of the High Court to prevent abuse of the process of any court and to secure the ends of justice are saved under which provision of the BNSS?","o":["Section 528","Section 483","Section 482","Section 531"],"a":0,"e":"Section 528 BNSS corresponds to Section 482 CrPC. Section 482 BNSS now deals with anticipatory bail, Section 483 with special powers of the High Court and Court of Session regarding bail, and Section 531 with repeal and savings."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"What safeguard does the proviso to Section 223(1) of the BNSS add before a Magistrate takes cognizance of an offence on a complaint?","o":["The District Magistrate's sanction is mandatory in every single case","The complainant must first obtain the prior leave of the High Court","A prior police enquiry report is mandatory in every single case","The Magistrate must give the accused an opportunity of being heard"],"a":3,"e":"Under Section 223 BNSS, no cognizance can be taken on a complaint without giving the accused an opportunity of being heard. This was not part of Section 200 CrPC. A separate rule applies to complaints against public servants for acts in discharge of official duty."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Where general words follow an enumeration of specific words belonging to a class, the general words are construed as limited to things of the same kind. This rule of interpretation is","o":["expressio unius est exclusio alterius","noscitur a sociis","generalia specialibus non derogant","ejusdem generis, the rule of the same class"],"a":3,"e":"Ejusdem generis limits general words to the genus of the preceding specific words. Noscitur a sociis derives the meaning of a word from its associates, and generalia specialibus non derogant says a special law prevails over a general law."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Which of the following is an implied CONDITION, and not merely a warranty, under Section 14 of the Sale of Goods Act, 1930?","o":["That the goods are fit for the buyer's purpose in all cases","That the seller has a right to sell the goods","That the goods are free from any charge in favour of a third party","That the buyer shall enjoy quiet possession of the goods"],"a":1,"e":"Section 14(a) implies a condition that the seller has a right to sell (or will have it when property is to pass). Freedom from encumbrances and quiet possession are implied warranties under Section 14(b) and (c). Fitness for purpose is implied under Section 16(1) only where the buyer relies on the seller's skill or judgment."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, the solatium payable in addition to the market value of the land is","o":["one hundred per cent of the compensation","fifty per cent of the compensation","thirty per cent of the compensation","two hundred per cent of the compensation"],"a":0,"e":"The Act provides solatium equal to 100% of the compensation under Section 30. The Land Acquisition Act, 1894 provided only 30%."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Mohd. Ahmed Khan v Shah Bano Begum (1985), the Supreme Court held that a divorced Muslim woman","o":["may claim maintenance only from the Wakf Board of her State, not from her husband","may claim maintenance only until the end of the iddat period and not beyond it","who cannot maintain herself may claim maintenance under Section 125 CrPC beyond iddat","may claim only the amount of mehr and nothing further from her former husband"],"a":2,"e":"Shah Bano held that Section 125 CrPC, a secular provision, applies to all women irrespective of religion. The Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986 followed, which Danial Latifi v Union of India (2001) upheld by reading it down. In Mohd. Abdul Samad v State of Telangana (2024), the Court affirmed that a divorced Muslim woman can claim maintenance under Section 125 CrPC (now Section 144 BNSS)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"In Hohfeld's scheme of jural relations, the jural correlative of a 'power' is","o":["no-right","duty","disability","liability"],"a":3,"e":"Hohfeld's correlatives are right-duty, privilege-no-right, power-liability and immunity-disability. His opposites are right-no-right, privilege-duty, power-disability and immunity-liability."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Muslim law, the wife's right to dower (mahr)","o":["exists even if no amount was fixed at the time of marriage","arises only if the marriage has been consummated by the parties","arises only if it is specified in a written nikahnama","is forfeited if the wife herself seeks a dissolution of marriage"],"a":0,"e":"Dower is an obligation on the husband imposed by law as a mark of respect for the wife. If no amount is fixed, the wife is entitled to 'proper dower'. Where a divorce occurs before consummation, half of the specified dower is payable."}]};
